WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.276
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.174 --> 00:00:05.340
HODIE MEYERS: Hey, everyone.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.380
We're really excited
to have you at Next.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:10.104
I'm Hodie Meyers, Project
Manager on Drive Platform.

00:00:10.104 --> 00:00:12.270
STEVE BAZYL: And I'm Steve
Basil, Developer Programs

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:14.759
Engineer for G Suite APIs.

00:00:14.759 --> 00:00:16.175
HODIE MEYERS: And
we're here today

00:00:16.175 --> 00:00:19.414
to talk about something that
every enterprise is facing.

00:00:19.414 --> 00:00:21.830
You'll always be growing your
wings, getting new people up

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:23.660
and productive.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.030
New team members need to get
access to all the existing

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:28.280
files, and when
people leave, you

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.300
want all their
work to disappear.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.180
The faster and smoother
you can achieve

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:36.150
this, the faster you get
employees up and productive.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.300
STEVE BAZYL: And that's
why we created Team Drives.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.410
Team Drives extends Drive with
safe, easy-to-manage shared

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:43.730
spaces for teams.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:46.400
And starting today,
Team Drives is supported

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:47.909
by the Google Drive API.

00:00:47.909 --> 00:00:49.700
We have a couple of
our partners here today

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:51.710
to talk about what they're
doing with that API

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:55.300
to help teams work
better together.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.910
HODIE MEYERS: In enterprise
and large organizations,

00:00:57.910 --> 00:00:59.590
the needs for drive
and drive apps

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:01.780
have evolved over
the last years.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:04.680
Most work is done in groups.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:09.020
When Drive and the Drive API
were originally designed,

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:12.460
the vision was on a
single user sharing files

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:17.470
on a one-off basis, all the
file ownership permissions

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:18.910
and engineering
optimizations were

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:21.886
centered on this one use case.

00:01:21.886 --> 00:01:24.010
So as a result, you could
get into confusing states

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:25.850
when you're
collaborating on files,

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:29.560
such as different people
having different views

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.446
on the same folder.

00:01:31.446 --> 00:01:33.820
For example, I shared the
presentation folder with Steve,

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:36.070
I had some files
in there for him.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:37.870
But I didn't realize
those files had

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.450
more restrictive permissions
than the folder itself.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:43.270
So Steve never even saw that
these files were in there,

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:45.760
and I didn't realize he didn't
see them until he asked me

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:46.950
for them weeks later.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:51.480
Team Drives is addressing this.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:56.229
The Team Drive looks the same
to everyone in the Team Drive.

00:01:56.229 --> 00:01:57.770
It's extremely
important to make sure

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:00.500
that all your team members have
access to all the information

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:01.367
they need.

00:02:01.367 --> 00:02:03.200
STEVE BAZYL: So let's
talk a little bit more

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.000
about that concretely.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.890
To ensure that
everyone in the team

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:09.289
gets a consistent view of
all the items in there,

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:11.630
we had to make some changes
to the permission model

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:14.300
and the organization model.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.150
So one of the more
notable changes

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:22.190
is a file can exist in either
my Drive or Team Drives,

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:23.840
but not both.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.700
When a file is in
Team Drives, it's

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.150
owned by the team itself,
not any individual user, not

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:31.980
the person who created the file.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.600
Those files also have a very
consistent location as files

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.910
and Team Drives could
only ever have one parent.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:41.090
So everybody knows where
every file is and they see

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:45.050
all files in the same place.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:48.470
To ensure that everyone
has a consistent view,

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:51.050
everyone who's a member of the
Team Drive can see all items.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:53.150
They may not all be able
to write those items.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:55.700
It just depends on the role.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:57.650
And one of the things
we've heard a lot

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:01.070
is people getting confused
when somebody deletes or moves

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:02.720
a file that's in
a shared folder,

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.891
and they don't realize that
somebody is using that file.

00:03:05.891 --> 00:03:07.640
And all of a sudden,
all these other users

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.480
don't know what happened.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.360
In Team Drives, we made
moving and deleting

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:12.320
a lot more restricted.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:15.350
We created a new role
called Organizer,

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:17.570
and Organizers are the only
members of the Team Drive

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:19.500
that can actually move
or delete content.

00:03:21.839 --> 00:03:23.380
HODIE MEYERS: But
sometimes you don't

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:25.210
want to add people
to your Team Drive,

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:26.793
but you still want
to give them access

00:03:26.793 --> 00:03:29.560
to individual files
within the Team Drive.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:32.260
For example, we created a
video demoing Team Drives.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:34.750
When we wanted to share with
folks among the company,

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:37.330
we didn't want to add
them to our Team Drive.

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:39.970
They were not part of our
team and all the internal docs

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:42.050
were of no relevance to them.

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:44.440
We just wanted to give them
access to this one file.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:47.830
So in addition to
Team Drive membership,

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:50.080
we have the concept
of file access,

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.600
giving users access
to individual files

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.480
within the Team Drive
without sharing anything

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:57.920
about the Team Drive itself.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.990
So when people saw the video of
our demo, they saw the video,

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:04.250
but they did not see any
information on our Team Drive

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:06.350
name, the people
in the Team Drive,

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:08.770
or the Team Drive folder
structure, because none of that

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:09.670
was relevant to them.

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:12.820
So none of that was shared.

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:16.810
And finally, the old drive
model could get confusing.

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:19.420
When you were collaborating
on the same file

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:22.450
and different people had the
same file on different folders.

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:24.100
Let's say I had
this presentation

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:26.080
in my Presentations
folder and Steve had it

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:27.520
in his Next folder.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:29.776
If Steve was sharing
his Next folder with me,

00:04:29.776 --> 00:04:31.900
I would have the same file
in two different places,

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:34.690
getting confused over which
one is the real one or are they

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:37.450
duplicates or copies.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:39.596
To make collaboration
less confusing,

00:04:39.596 --> 00:04:43.600
Team Drive files have
exactly one parent.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:45.340
That means that
every Team Drive file

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:48.430
belongs to a single Team
Drive and a single location

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:49.400
within this Team Drive.

00:04:51.874 --> 00:04:53.540
STEVE BAZYL: All of
these changes change

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:56.420
how the Drive API
works with Team Drives.

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:59.990
To avoid breaking applications,
we took an opt-in approach.

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:02.240
That means if you want to
work with content in Drive

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.380
or manage Team
Drives themselves,

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:06.920
you need to make changes
to your application.

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:10.640
If you don't, your
app won't break,

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.280
you just won't be able
to access that content.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.130
Before you begin though,
be aware that Team Drives

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:19.550
is only available for G Suite
Business and Enterprise.

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:21.590
In order to play
with Team Drives,

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:24.292
learn about the features,
and experiment with the API,

00:05:24.292 --> 00:05:26.750
you'll need to have a domain
with either of those additions

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:29.210
in order to try
these features out.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:32.360
That said, the opt-in
approach starts

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.640
with including an
extra query parameter

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:36.380
in most of your API calls.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:39.680
And this is appropriately
named supportsTeamDrives

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.150
and you set it to true.

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:44.300
This is an acknowledgement
to us that your application

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:47.330
is ready to handle some of
the behavioral differences

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:50.970
that you'll experience with
content in Team Drives.

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:52.460
Of course, your
application needs

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:54.860
to be able to handle
those differences.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:58.490
And this includes things
such as response fields that

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:01.490
aren't populated on
Team Drive content,

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:05.630
the awareness of new roles,
like Organizer, and restrictions

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:10.040
on operations such as setting
[? parenths ?] so that you

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:12.800
can only have one
inside a Team Drive.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:14.690
If you don't set
this parameter, we'll

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:16.951
just pretend that the files
that you're asking for

00:06:16.951 --> 00:06:18.575
or any files in Team
Drive don't exist.

00:06:18.575 --> 00:06:21.189
And if you try to get
it, you will get a 404,

00:06:21.189 --> 00:06:23.480
just like any other file that
you don't have access to.

00:06:26.890 --> 00:06:29.260
This, of course, is really
just the tip of the iceberg

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:32.950
and a bit of an
oversimplification.

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:34.930
Fortunately, though,
many apps won't

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:36.410
need to do much more than this.

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:38.050
In fact, I was
just playing around

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:39.640
with one of our
sample applications.

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:42.430
It was a simple text editor that
we had built a couple of years

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:46.340
ago, and literally the changes
were about three lines of code.

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:48.730
So in that case, it
was very, very simple

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:50.800
for me to support Team Drives.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:52.990
For other applications
though, the changes

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:55.030
can get complicated
pretty quickly.

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:57.550
If you deal a lot
with permissions,

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:02.170
change logs, or do a lot
of file organization,

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:04.660
you're likely going to have
a lot more changes to think

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:05.820
about.

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:07.960
We created a guide
in our documentation

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.930
that details all the behavioral
changes that you'll experience

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:13.690
with the Drive API so
you can plan your work

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:15.528
and assess the
impact appropriately.

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:19.180
HODIE MEYERS: So
far, we've told you

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:22.450
a lot about what we've
built. But you're probably

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:24.790
really interested in what
you can do with this building

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:26.710
and how you can fill it.

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:28.270
So for the Team
Drives launch, we've

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:29.978
worked with our top
partners to make sure

00:07:29.978 --> 00:07:32.944
that Team Drives API
is working for them

00:07:32.944 --> 00:07:34.360
and that their
apps are supporting

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:36.660
Team Drives at launch.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:38.880
We have five partners
here with us today

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.620
to tell us all about how they've
made use of the Team Drives API

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:44.570
and maybe share some
tricks what they've

00:07:44.570 --> 00:07:47.090
learned working with it.

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:50.630
First, we have Paul
Lees from CloudMigrator.

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:52.400
PAUL LEES: Hi, my
name is Paul Lees

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:57.280
and I'm the co-founder and CTO
of Cloud Technology Solutions,

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:00.620
and we're the developers
of the CloudMigrator.

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:04.790
CloudMigrator is the world's
leading migration platform

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:06.440
for Google G Suite.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:10.280
We've helped organizations
migrate emails, calendars,

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:16.760
contacts, and files to the
Google G Suite environment.

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:20.900
We've moved over 5.5
million individuals

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:24.050
and over 3 petabytes of data.

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:27.920
And now we're helping
organizations move files

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:33.770
from systems such Box,
Dropbox, OneDrive, SharePoint,

00:08:33.770 --> 00:08:36.770
local file systems,
and even Google Drive

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:40.710
shared folders to Team Drive.

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:43.384
We've been working with the
Google APIs for many years

00:08:43.384 --> 00:08:45.300
and we've been involved
in some of the largest

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:47.050
migrations to date.

00:08:47.050 --> 00:08:50.130
So for us, like Steve
mentioned, moving to Team

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:53.780
Drives was a fairly
trivial task.

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:56.950
However, I'd like to
focus more on what

00:08:56.950 --> 00:08:58.750
we've done with one
of our customers

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:03.710
that we've helped to
move to Team Drives.

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:05.810
Let me introduce you to Veolia.

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:08.390
Veolia is a large organization.

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:12.080
They specialize in
water, waste management,

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:13.730
and energy services.

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:19.310
Veolia have over 170,000
employees around the world.

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:24.580
115,000 of those have
a G Suite account.

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:27.880
Veolia moved to
Google back in 2013,

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:30.190
and they have a very
well-established Google

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:32.200
environment and ecosystem.

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:39.290
Veolia have made extensive
use of Google Drive

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:42.780
with over 18
million files shared

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:45.210
inside their organization.

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:47.970
Collaboration has always
been key to the way

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:50.640
that Veolia work.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:54.390
Individuals get together to
form ad hoc groups, growing

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:56.760
and shrinking as
the need requires.

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:00.450
These teams have utilized
Google Drive and Google Docs.

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:03.950
However, Veolia have started to
experience some of the issues

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:07.460
that Hodie mentioned around
file inconsistencies.

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:11.640
And specifically offboarding
uses, where a manual process

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:13.830
will have to be
performed in order

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:17.340
to transfer ownership
of files for users

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:19.090
who are being offboarded.

00:10:19.090 --> 00:10:22.020
So the release of Team Drives
and the move to Team Drives

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:26.440
was absolutely
critical for Veolia.

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:30.490
So working with the global
IS&amp;T team of Veolia,

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:34.600
we set about implementing
a Team Drives pilot.

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:37.960
Using the CloudMigrator,
we analyzed

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:41.320
existing Google shared folders.

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:45.040
First, we had to identify
the most appropriate users

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:48.730
inside these shared
folders to make sure

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:52.210
that they would be able to
benefit from collaborating

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:54.750
inside a Team Drive.

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:58.580
We then needed to get a
360 degree view of the file

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:00.950
sharing inside these folders.

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:05.610
We needed to make sure that
every user had the same view

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:09.250
of these file shares.

00:11:09.250 --> 00:11:10.840
We also needed to
make sure there

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:13.650
wasn't any accidental
sharing permissions.

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:16.420
Maybe those didn't need
to be passed over when

00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:17.800
we moved to Team Drives.

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:20.800
Could the users
see the same files?

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:24.550
And because when you're using
the API you're migrating

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:27.520
or you're focusing
on a specific user,

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:30.100
you're performing a
migration as a user,

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:32.380
we need to identify
which user we need to do

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:34.030
the migration on behalf of.

00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:36.250
Because if we perform the
migration for somebody

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:38.899
who doesn't have access
to all of the files,

00:11:38.899 --> 00:11:40.690
then we're going to
miss some of that data.

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:43.750
So again, the analytics
is key to how that works.

00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:55.380
So the result, seamless
integration and migration

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:56.850
with CloudMigrator.

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:59.910
The users can now
work on existing files

00:11:59.910 --> 00:12:04.230
while still taking
advantage of Team Drives.

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:07.770
And while this event is really
a technology conference,

00:12:07.770 --> 00:12:13.530
it's important to understand
the impact that we can have

00:12:13.530 --> 00:12:16.950
to users on their daily lives.

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:23.070
So I'm going to leave you with
a statement from the Veolia CTO

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:27.047
and on why he feels Team
Drive is so important.

00:12:33.712 --> 00:12:36.170
STEVE BAZYL: Thanks and thanks
for making it so much easier

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:38.616
to migrate to Team Drives.

00:12:38.616 --> 00:12:41.962
[APPLAUSE]

00:12:44.161 --> 00:12:45.160
HODIE MEYERS: Thank you.

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:48.781
And next up is Nate
Collum from BetterCloud.

00:12:48.781 --> 00:12:52.677
[APPLAUSE]

00:12:54.489 --> 00:12:56.780
NATE COLLUM: If I can figure
out how to make this work.

00:12:56.780 --> 00:12:57.280
OK, great.

00:12:57.280 --> 00:12:58.820
Thank you, Hodie.

00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:01.610
I'm very excited to be at
Next and to share with you how

00:13:01.610 --> 00:13:05.990
BetterCloud will support the
management and adoption of Team

00:13:05.990 --> 00:13:08.570
Drive for your organization.

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:12.290
BetterCloud is the first
ever unified SaaS management

00:13:12.290 --> 00:13:16.280
platform, enabling IT
to centralize, automate,

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:21.180
and govern users and data across
all of their SaaS environments.

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:23.510
Let me talk a little bit
about how we do that.

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:29.000
As a SaaS-powered organization
grows in SaaS app adoption

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:35.720
and users, manual
administration for these users

00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:38.060
inside these native
admin consoles

00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:41.390
becomes an unscalable
burden for IT,

00:13:41.390 --> 00:13:45.230
and it also creates a security
risk for your organization.

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:47.960
BetterCloud centralizes
the functions

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:50.720
available in these
disparate SaaS admin

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:54.110
consoles into a single
point of control.

00:13:54.110 --> 00:13:57.860
BetterCloud connects directly
to your SaaS applications APIs

00:13:57.860 --> 00:14:01.430
and ingests information
about your organization

00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:05.420
and then we normalize that
data into buckets, like users,

00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:08.120
and groups, and
data, and resources.

00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:11.840
And we present
those in data grids,

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:13.910
and reporting, and
alerts, and insights.

00:14:13.910 --> 00:14:17.660
And we allow you to take
action on those data objects,

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:20.780
individual or one-off,
and of course we

00:14:20.780 --> 00:14:24.050
give you the ability to
create automated workflows

00:14:24.050 --> 00:14:26.750
to streamline management
for these SaaS apps.

00:14:26.750 --> 00:14:30.530
Again, all from a
single interface.

00:14:30.530 --> 00:14:33.020
BetterCloud is all in
when it comes to Google.

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:38.090
We're the number one admin app
on the G Suite marketplace.

00:14:38.090 --> 00:14:41.480
Since our inception over
the last five years,

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:49.490
we've served over 3,000
customers with 4 million users.

