00:18:48.450 --> 00:18:51.390
With the advent of
cloud and mobile,

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:53.400
more and more
people are not bound

00:18:53.400 --> 00:18:57.030
by the four corners of
going to a company every day

00:18:57.030 --> 00:18:58.440
and working there.

00:18:58.440 --> 00:19:01.830
So with that,
security has obviously

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:03.960
had to have to change.

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:06.060
Let's talk a little
bit about why.

00:19:06.060 --> 00:19:08.940
We have IaaS, PaaS,
and SaaS applications

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:10.770
more readily available.

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:14.010
And we're going to
these applications

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:16.380
because they enhance
our productivity,

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:19.020
they're more cost
effective, but also we

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:22.920
have our on-premise data
moving to the public cloud,

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:26.440
critical data moving
to the public cloud.

00:19:26.440 --> 00:19:29.340
The second thing is
with coffee shop IT.

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:34.140
We're no longer having
to work in the office

00:19:34.140 --> 00:19:35.250
and log on to the VPN.

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:43.500
This can leave us a little bit
more vulnerable to attacks,

00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:47.370
and companies can lack that
visibility and protection.

00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:50.100
And the third thing is with
bring-your-own-device and then

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:53.730
turn out of things, there's
more unmanaged devices going

00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:56.130
on your network.

00:19:56.130 --> 00:20:02.990
So with CloudLock, it's
a new security vector

00:20:02.990 --> 00:20:05.690
that has been established
because of these things.

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:09.590
Our concern now is what
data is in the cloud, who

00:20:09.590 --> 00:20:13.850
is accessing it, and what are
they doing with that data.

00:20:13.850 --> 00:20:18.850
And CASB is the new security
vector that's protecting it.

00:20:18.850 --> 00:20:24.320
And CloudLock has been working
with Google since 2007,

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:26.180
protecting Google
Drive environments.

00:20:26.180 --> 00:20:29.590
We protect SaaS, PaaS,
and IaaS applications,

00:20:29.590 --> 00:20:34.040
and protect the user's data and
apps within these applications.

00:20:39.910 --> 00:20:42.370
Hodie and Steve mentioned
some of the challenges

00:20:42.370 --> 00:20:45.940
before on a visibility
level, and I

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:48.340
want to talk a little bit
about some of the challenges

00:20:48.340 --> 00:20:51.370
that we had on a security level.

00:20:51.370 --> 00:20:55.750
So what we did was we applied
CloudLock's User Behavior

00:20:55.750 --> 00:20:58.960
Entity Analytics to Team Drive.

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:02.440
And so for simplicity's
sake, let's take company

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:05.060
ABC and their finance team.

00:21:05.060 --> 00:21:10.660
So the finance team's
files are company assets.

00:21:10.660 --> 00:21:12.220
They're not personal.

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:16.810
And before, the finance teams
files were attached to a user,

00:21:16.810 --> 00:21:20.350
and sharing was was based
on specific names, which

00:21:20.350 --> 00:21:22.510
can be prone to error.

00:21:22.510 --> 00:21:26.410
Another thing that happened
was getting visibility

00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:30.160
over what content was in these
files and classifying them.

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:34.330
So files could reside
in multiple drives,

00:21:34.330 --> 00:21:38.410
and you could use CloudLock's
DLP policies to classify them,

00:21:38.410 --> 00:21:43.930
but you still had
sensitive information

00:21:43.930 --> 00:21:46.480
distributed across many users.

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:52.270
And to get the big picture
view for company ABC,

00:21:52.270 --> 00:21:54.850
you had to take the
sum of all their files

00:21:54.850 --> 00:21:59.240
and their sensitive
information to get an overview.

00:21:59.240 --> 00:22:01.280
With Team Drive,
this is changed.

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:06.950
You can specify a container.

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:17.480
You can label a container and
put a specific team around it.

00:22:17.480 --> 00:22:20.240
So what we're doing now
is, the finance team,

00:22:20.240 --> 00:22:23.120
all they need to do is
establish a circle of trust

00:22:23.120 --> 00:22:25.280
and now the sharing
is centralized.

00:22:25.280 --> 00:22:28.550
And the focus now is to make
sure that our circle of trust

00:22:28.550 --> 00:22:30.080
doesn't get violated.

00:22:30.080 --> 00:22:34.480
And we can use CloudLock
to monitor that container.

00:22:34.480 --> 00:22:36.690
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:39.570
going to monitor all the user
activities for the finance

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:42.240
files, and we can
have a new confidence

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:44.700
in knowing that ABC's
finance documents are

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:47.070
in one specific location.

00:22:47.070 --> 00:22:51.360
And what's notable here is
once data categorization is

00:22:51.360 --> 00:22:53.320
established, and
you've established

00:22:53.320 --> 00:22:57.150
your circle of trust,
companies can now

00:22:57.150 --> 00:23:01.380
put sensitive information in
the cloud and have it contained

00:23:01.380 --> 00:23:02.790
and actively monitor it.

00:23:05.770 --> 00:23:08.910
So what is monitor
contain and visibility?

00:23:08.910 --> 00:23:10.380
What does that all mean?

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:11.850
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:23:11.850 --> 00:23:15.450
going to actively monitor the
team's activities and Team

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:18.870
Drive, and then detect
for nonmember access.

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:22.230
We can also detect for Team
Drive membership changes

00:23:22.230 --> 00:23:26.980
in case a nonmember gets access
to the sensitive information

00:23:26.980 --> 00:23:29.520
and we can see what
actions they have taken

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:32.070
and what access they've had.

00:23:32.070 --> 00:23:34.980
On top of that, we can
actively receive notifications

00:23:34.980 --> 00:23:37.740
when a violation does occur.

00:23:37.740 --> 00:23:39.900
Google does a great
job with security.

00:23:39.900 --> 00:23:41.610
However, to be the
most secure, you

00:23:41.610 --> 00:23:43.620
need to take a layered
approach, and this

00:23:43.620 --> 00:23:47.850
is one additional layer to
add to your security stack.

00:23:56.600 --> 00:24:00.850
At CloudLock, we serve some
of Google's largest enterprise

00:24:00.850 --> 00:24:04.840
clients, so we've had
some pretty complex use

00:24:04.840 --> 00:24:06.580
cases to work through.

00:24:06.580 --> 00:24:10.370
I want to tell you a little
bit about that today.

00:24:10.370 --> 00:24:13.420
So two examples of
challenges that we've had.

00:24:13.420 --> 00:24:16.015
The first one is around
the definition of a user.

00:24:18.580 --> 00:24:20.680
In CloudLock, when
you're creating a policy,

00:24:20.680 --> 00:24:23.980
everything sort of revolves
around the user and the actions

00:24:23.980 --> 00:24:25.630
that user takes.

00:24:25.630 --> 00:24:28.510
So we had to reevaluate what
an owner means when we're

00:24:28.510 --> 00:24:30.580
looking at actions
that people are taking

00:24:30.580 --> 00:24:34.720
and what that means
for policy enforcement.

00:24:34.720 --> 00:24:36.970
The second challenge
that we came across

00:24:36.970 --> 00:24:43.330
was to get a full picture
of you, which you'll

00:24:43.330 --> 00:24:46.540
need to do if you're
securing your environment,

00:24:46.540 --> 00:24:48.880
is you need to be able
to look at all the Team

00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:51.640
Drives within that environment.

00:24:51.640 --> 00:24:55.520
And when we first
started working together,

00:24:55.520 --> 00:24:56.770
there was no way to do that.

00:24:56.770 --> 00:24:57.610
It was impossible.

00:24:57.610 --> 00:24:59.560
So we worked with
Hodie and Steve

00:24:59.560 --> 00:25:01.810
to actually create
an administrator API

00:25:01.810 --> 00:25:05.140
so that we could look at all
Team Drives in an environment.

00:25:05.140 --> 00:25:08.380
So just some things to
consider when you are working

00:25:08.380 --> 00:25:11.950
with the Team Drive API.

00:25:11.950 --> 00:25:14.150
Security should
always be a priority.

00:25:14.150 --> 00:25:17.080
However, the user
experience is also

00:25:17.080 --> 00:25:19.750
very critical, and
negatively impacting

00:25:19.750 --> 00:25:23.590
that user experience can
force users and companies

00:25:23.590 --> 00:25:26.410
to seek alternative solutions.

00:25:26.410 --> 00:25:31.540
Some cloud applications make
you put a proxy or a gateway

00:25:31.540 --> 00:25:36.310
in front of the solution in
order to protect and enhance

00:25:36.310 --> 00:25:37.450
security.

00:25:37.450 --> 00:25:41.230
And because Google takes
an API-first mentality,

00:25:41.230 --> 00:25:43.960
it makes it very easy for
our customers and vendors

00:25:43.960 --> 00:25:46.540
like us to work with Google.

00:25:46.540 --> 00:25:48.560
It's been great
working with the team.

00:25:48.560 --> 00:25:51.160
They've been very responsive
to us and our questions

00:25:51.160 --> 00:25:56.490
around the API, and we're
really excited to release

00:25:56.490 --> 00:25:59.760
this [INAUDIBLE] for Team
Drive because our customers are

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:02.040
